  <title>
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">Canine Detection to Strengthen and Replicate Anti-Trafficking Capacity in Key Countries</narrative>
  </title>
  <description type="1">
   <narrative xml:lang="EN">The purpose of this project is to build the anti-wildlife trafficking capacity in to key range, transit and consumer states: Malawi and Kyrgyzstan, by improving, expanding and establishing canine teams for wildlife law enforcement. Multiple species subjected to illicit wildlife trade will benefit from this work, including elephants, bears, pangolins, saiga, and snow leopard. Specific activities include: (1) tracking the activities, costs, processes of establishment and the workflows of canine programs to establish a set of detection dog resources for other wildlife agencies; (2) reviewing the legal foundation for canine-based law enforcement of wildlife laws in key trafficking countries; (3) producing a Best Practices and Feasibility Report on the application of detection dogs to combat wildlife trafficking; (4) Improving the husbandry and training for the Malawi Police Service wildlife detection dogs while expanding their capability to screen at roadblocks, rail and airports and at board crossings; (5) expand detection dog activities in Kyrgyzstan to include wildlife products.</narrative>
  </description>
